OtisBench
Copyright Faraday contributors.

OtisBench incorporates the Faraday Manufacturing Benchmark manufacturing engine,
case catalogs and authored assets. Internal Python namespaces and versioned
wire protocols retain their Faraday names for compatibility.

Faraday Manufacturing Benchmark
Copyright Faraday contributors.

The manufacturing execution engine in src/faraday_industrial_benchmark and the
selected execution tasks derive from Faraday Industrial Benchmark, revision
370b7a2f927ed94c6ef4413823c4aafefab70971.
https://github.com/Faraday-Cage-AI/faraday-industrial-benchmark
Engine code: Apache License 2.0 (LICENSE). Task content: CC BY 4.0 (DATA_LICENSE).

The optional ERP compatibility adapter loads selected ERP-Bench tasks from
agentic-labs/erp-bench, revision ceba3880af555129b5278e056a0c20f2fb5a0ba9.
https://github.com/agentic-labs/erp-bench
The pinned upstream LICENSE declares CC0 1.0 Universal. Original instructions,
scenario data, reference plans and verifiers retain explicit upstream provenance.
They are downloaded into evaluator caches, not counted as new Faraday-authored
cases. Odoo and other runtime software retain their respective licenses; no Odoo
server code or Docker image is bundled in this Python package.

The optional real hydraulic extension downloads the Condition monitoring of
hydraulic systems dataset by Nikolai Helwig, Eliseo Pignanelli and Andreas
Schuetze (2015), UCI Machine Learning Repository, DOI 10.24432/C5CW21.
https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/447/condition+monitoring+of+hydraulic+systems
Dataset license: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0).
The raw source archive is pinned by SHA-256 in scripts/hydraulic_benchmark.py.
Faraday's changes are physical-channel selection, summary features, grouped
partitions, local baseline evaluation and reporting. The measurements and
experimental condition labels remain upstream data, adding zero native cases.

The optional visual inspection extension uses the Visual Anomaly (VisA) dataset
from Yang Zou, Jongheon Jeong, Latha Pemula, Dongqing Zhang and Onkar Dabeer:
SPot-the-Difference Self-Supervised Pre-training for Anomaly Detection and
Segmentation, ECCV 2022. https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.14315
https://github.com/amazon-science/spot-diff
VisA images and masks: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0).
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
Pinned source code/metadata snapshots retain their upstream Apache-2.0 notices.
Faraday's modifications are RGB/mask resampling, opaque identifiers, grouped
normal-only fit/calibration partitions, local statistical baselines and metrics.
Original source images/masks are retained unchanged in optional caches. They
are not bundled in the native wheel or counted as Faraday-authored workflows.
